{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_15-11-32-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run; python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor e in s['interpretations']:\n    if e['id']=='9a11dc5e5af748bc87cf9c6f80463456-interpretation-0':\n        print(json.dumps(e['rationale'],ensure_ascii=False))\n        print('---COUNTER')\n        print(json.dumps(e['counterevidence'],ensure_ascii=False,indent=1))\n        print('---LIMITS')\n        print(json.dumps(e['limitations'],ensure_ascii=False,indent=1))\n        print('---EXEC')\n        print(json.dumps(e['executions'],ensure_ascii=False))\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"\"Current account: the anomaly is resolved and it is a defect distinct from the fabricated-self-ballot one. Receipt 81a44ff62122417db3cc5697fc5303f6 (fixed experiment 0757162a4aa64ef4a58a90002641eb27-experiment) shows the owner holding its command X at status ACCEPTED before its own recovery (index 0), the empty acceptor at status NONE after the Accept (index 3), and, after the owner recovered its own instance and the three PrepareReplies were delivered, an instance left at status PREACCEPTED with an empty command list plus bookkeeping whose candidate command is an empty slice (index 4: inst_cmds_len 0, inst_status 1, lb_cmds_len 0, lb_status 1, lb_appended 1, lb_nacks 0), with no command proposed afterwards (index 5) and the same values in the second run (indices 9-11); comparison 0 and comparison 1 are both violated with witnesses 5 and 11. The mechanism is now sourced: a reply from a replica that holds no command carries a nil command slice, the decoder allocates it with make([]state.Command, alen1) so the receiver sees a non-nil empty slice (epaxos/defs.go:228-235), the adoption loop copies that empty slice into lb.cmds because its VBallot equals the running candidate ballot (epaxos/epaxos.go:1239-1246), and the sub-case 5/6 fallback substitutes state.NOOP() only when lb.cmds is nil (epaxos/epaxos.go:1307-1311), which an empty slice is not - so startPhase1 is called with an empty command batch and the instance becomes PREACCEPTED with Cmds of length zero (epaxos/epaxos.go:1291-1296, 751-779). That also explains why the probe recorded no PreAccept command (proposed_cmd_op -1 was the empty batch, not an absent proposal) and why the probe's two runs coincided: the empty slice wins in both delivery orders because the loop adopts ties with >= and the empty reply is adopted whatever its position, the last delivery being irrelevant. Consequences for the captured package: the instance is proposed and can be decided with no command at all, replacing the command the owner itself held; executeCommands accepts an empty non-nil batch because its guard is a nil test, so the instance executes as a no-op rather than stalling; and the client re-offer path that protects the fabricated-NOOP case does not fire here, because handleCommit tests for exactly one command with operation NONE (epaxos/epaxos.go:1090-1099), which an empty batch never satisfies. A second receipt measures the client-side consequence of exactly that decision: receipt 098ab4425325464192aa558be557f0ce (fixed experiment 49b316fdc67c459fb374500fb7f26514-experiment) attaches a real client proposal (command id 91) to the owner's own instance, that instance's own recovery decides the empty batch, and both declared comparisons are violated with complete witness 4 - the decided instance holds a command list of length zero while the owner held command X before (index 0: status 3, cmd_op 1) and the owner's pending request is never re-offered (requeued 0 with pending_proposals 1 and reply_bytes 0), while the single-NOOP decision of the same family (receipt 893f6180a4514a8d9e20a1d5e6698542) re-queued its request and later completed it. The contrast is the re-offer guard's own condition: handleCommit requires exactly one command with operation NONE (epaxos/epaxos.go:1090-1099), which an empty batch never satisfies, so the empty-command decision drops the client's request with neither a reply nor a re-offer. The delivery-order question is now measured with three runs of the same reply set (receipt e89ecb076e8d4a78b174f848562ed77f, fixed experiment e8fb81e1cb0742118ce33f60e08a0de1-experiment): with the replies delivered as 2,4,3 the instance ends with no command (comparison 0 violated, witness 6); as 3,4,2 the same outcome (comparison 1 violated, witness 13); and as 3,2,4 - where the command-carrying reply is the second delivered - the instance keeps the owner's command (comparison 2 holds, evaluated 20). The mechanism is therefore not a simple last-reply-wins rule over the whole set but the timing of the quorum trigger: handlePrepareReply dispatches as soon as len(lb.prepareReplies) reaches SlowQuorumSize, which for one self reply and a five-replica configuration is two peer replies, and the candidate command is whatever the last adopted reply in that prefix carries. In this reply set two peers carry no command and one carries X, so two of the three delivery orders put an empty reply in the adopted position and lose the command, while the third order puts X there and keeps it. The adopted value therefore depends on arrival order among equally admissible replies rather than on their content, and the empty-slice handling is what makes the losing orders lose the command outright instead of substituting state.NOOP(). The earlier statement that the order variant was unmeasured is withdrawn; the state/command invariant entry is qualified rather than wrong: it holds for the command-carrying writers, while this path reaches the same status-without-command combination through an empty non-nil slice, which that entry's nil test does not cover.\"\n---COUNTER\n[\n \"The replacement needs a reply that carries no command at a value ballot at least as high as the recovering replica's candidate ballot; in this run that reply came from an acceptor that never pre-accepted, which required the accept broadcast to skip a failed peer, so the same reachability limit as the value-adoption finding applies.\",\n \"The owner's command is not re-offered by the captured package in this scenario, and that is now measured rather than derived: receipt 098ab4425325464192aa558be557f0ce shows the decided instance holding an empty command list, the pending proposal count at 1 and nothing on the owner's propose channel, so the client-visible completion depends on the client's own retry rather than on the re-offer path that covers the single-NOOP case.\",\n \"The outcome is order-dependent rather than inevitable: the third recorded order (3,2,4) kept the owner's command with the same reply set, so the empty-command loss is contingent on which reply is adopted last before the quorum trigger, not a property of the replies' content alone.\",\n \"Both runs came from one execution and one configuration; no independent receipt shows the same replacement.\",\n \"The measurement is bounded to the owner-as-recoverer case with one pending proposal; it does not show what a client library would do next, since no client process ran and the reply writer stayed empty.\",\n \"The earlier claim that the two first runs refuted order dependence is withdrawn: those runs did not apply an order at all, and the three-order receipt shows the opposite - the same message set yields two different decided commands depending only on arrival order.\"\n]\n---LIMITS\n[\n \"One configuration and instance (N=5, F=2, instance (0,0), one command, owner as recoverer); the scenario was not repeated with a non-owner recoverer or with several commands in a batch.\",\n \"Delivery order was exercised in three specific orders among the six permutations of the three peer replies; the two orders that lost the command and the one that kept it are recorded, but the full permutation space was not enumerated, and the trigger position (second peer reply) follows from the configuration rather than from an independent manipulation.\",\n \"The empty-command decision's execution-side effects (execution as a no-op because the batch is non-nil but empty, ordering of dependents) remain derived from source; no executor ran. The client-side effect is now measured for this scenario, including the absence of a re-offer and the empty reply writer.\",\n \"The decoding behaviour is stated for the PrepareReply decoder that this path uses; the claim that all RPC decoders allocate the slice the same way rests on the generated Marshal/Unmarshal pattern rather than on a run.\"\n]\n---EXEC\n{\"81a44ff62122417db3cc5697fc5303f6\": [0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 9, 10, 11], \"098ab4425325464192aa558be557f0ce\": [0, 2, 3, 4], \"e89ecb076e8d4a78b174f848562ed77f\": [6, 13, 20]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_15-11-32-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run; python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor e in s['knowledge']:\n    if e['id']=='bb3ff1d8a54a40c6a821a74647a72c21-knowledge-0':\n        print(json.dumps({k:e[k] for k in ('statement','conditions','executions')},ensure_ascii=False,indent=1))\nprint('=== QUESTION')\nfor q in s['investigations']:\n    if q['id'].startswith('0757162a'):\n        print(json.dumps({k:q.get(k) for k in ('id','version','question','hypothesis','discriminator','disposition','question_state')},ensure_ascii=False,indent=1))\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -c \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-16_15-11-32-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run; python3 -c \\\"\nimport json\ns=json.load(open('state.json'))\nfor e in s['knowledge']:\n    if e['id']=='bb3ff1d8a54a40c6a821a74647a72c21-knowledge-0':\n        print(json.dumps({k:e[k] for k in ('statement','conditions','executions')},ensure_ascii=False,indent=1))\nprint('=== QUESTION')\nfor q in s['investigations']:\n    if q['id'].startswith('0757162a'):\n        print(json.dumps({k:q.get(k) for k in ('id','version','question','hypothesis','discriminator','disposition','question_state')},ensure_ascii=False,indent=1))\n\\\"\"","aggregated_output":"{\n \"statement\": \"In the captured EPaxos package the value a recovery adopts is not protected by the quorum that holds it: four distinct mechanisms of the same recovery path each let a recovering replica end up proposing, and then deciding, a value other than the one its prepare replies carry. (a) startRecoveryForInstance fabricates a value ballot for its own empty state, so the adoption loop skips genuine replies (epaxos/epaxos.go:1145-1165, 1239-1246). (b) A reply whose value ballot is at most the fabricated one is ignored even when it reports the highest accepted value, so a NOOP replaces an accepted value, including one another live replica has already committed. (c) A reply that reports the instance COMMITTED is discarded once its status reaches lb.status, because the pre-accept guard then rejects the remaining replies, so the first recovery attempt stalls until the retry (epaxos/epaxos.go:870-881, 1255-1313). (d) A reply that carries no command decodes as a non-nil empty slice, which the sub-case 5/6 fallback passes to startPhase1 instead of state.NOOP() (epaxos/defs.go:228-235, epaxos/epaxos.go:1307-1311), and which command is adopted is fixed by the replies present when the slow-quorum trigger fires, so arrival order alone can decide whether the owner's command survives. Recovering replicas that already hold the value are not affected by (a)-(b), and the client re-offer protects the single-NOOP case but not the empty-slice case.\",\n \"conditions\": \"N=5, F=2 configuration of the captured snapshot with Thrifty on and IsLeader false at the recovering replica; the instance owner fails inside its accept window and the recovering replica holds the instance without its command, or (for the arrival-order variant) recovers its own instance while replies at the same value ballot differ.\",\n \"executions\": {\n  \"143e14592eea4c42b3c81044e3860348\": [\n   7,\n   8,\n   9,\n   11,\n   14,\n   16,\n   17,\n   19,\n   20,\n   21,\n   22,\n   23\n  ],\n  \"d80cbfd9f9a547639811aeacb19c0a30\": [\n   6,\n   10,\n   11,\n   16,\n   19,\n   21,\n   24,\n   25,\n   26,\n   27\n  ],\n  \"e89ecb076e8d4a78b174f848562ed77f\": [\n   6,\n   13,\n   20\n  ],\n  \"098ab4425325464192aa558be557f0ce\": [\n   3,\n   4\n  ]\n }\n}\n=== QUESTION\n{\n \"id\": \"0757162a4aa64ef4a58a90002641eb27-question-0\",\n \"version\": 2,\n \"question\": \"Can a recovery of an instance by its own alive owner drop the instance's command when some replies at the same value ballot carry no command, and does the outcome depend on the delivery order of those replies?\",\n \"hypothesis\": \"The adoption loop takes any reply whose VBallot is at least the running candidate ballot and copies its command into the candidate, so a reply at the same value ballot that carries no command can replace the command the owner itself contributed; because the dispatch fires when the appended replies reach the slow quorum, which reply is adopted last depends on arrival order, and with two empty replies against one command-carrying reply two of the three exercised orders lose the command while the third keeps it.\",\n \"discriminator\": \"Whether the remaining three permutations of the three peer replies behave as the trigger position predicts, and whether a scenario with more command-carrying replies than empty ones flips the outcome.\",\n \"disposition\": \"explained\",\n \"question_state\": null\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
